  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon model 500mb height analysis and GOES water \nvapor imagery showed an upper level high pressure over the Gulf \nCoast states extending weak ridging into the Upper Great Lakes. Up \nstream a trough was over British Colombia, approaching the Pacific \nNorthwest. Shortwaves were embedded in the flow ahead of the trough \nfrom the Midwest north into Manitoba, resulting in areas of showers \nand thunderstorms. The UP had remained dry so far with a decaying \nMCS over MO/IL/IN interrupting moist transport northwards. Skies \nranged from partly cloudy in the east to cloudy in the west. Surface \nobservations showed temperatures in the upper 70s to low 80s in the \neast but cooler, low 70s, in the west where clouds were keeping \ntemperatures down. Winds were from the east and southeast 5-10 mph.\n\nThe chance for showers will increase tonight as a cold front \napproaches the UP. The best dynamics with the associated shortwave \nwill stay north of the area, thus forcing will overall be lacking. \nInstability is also meager with poor lapse rates and skinny CAPE \nprofiles. PWATs do approach 1.75\" as the front moves through the \narea with dewpoints rising into the upper 60s to near 70 by Sunday \nmorning. Therefor, any thunderstorms that do form will be capable of \nheavy downpours. That being said, heavier downpours will be \nrelatively isolated with most of the UP will see precipitation \namounts struggling to approach that of a wetting rain (0.10\"). The \nfront will exit the eastern UP on Sunday morning. Immediately behind \nthe front a period of light rain or drizzle is possible in the \nwestern U.P. with upslope westerly flow. Skies should begin to clear \nduring the afternoon as drier air filters into the area from the \nnorthwest. Higher dewpoints tonight will keep the airmass on the \nmuggier side with lows in the 60s.\n\nNorthwest flow aloft is expected for Sunday night through much of \nthe week with a seasonable, comfortable airmass providing highs in \nthe mid to upper 70s and lows in the 40s and 50s. Another cold front \nwill move through the U.P. Monday night into Tuesday bringing the \nchance for showers and thunderstorms. The GEFS and deterministic \nmedium range guidance are trending faster with the front, moving\nit through Monday night into Tuesday morning. This scenario \nwould limit thunderstorm chances as the frontal passage will not\nalign with diurnal instability and large scale forcing and \nmoisture return look marginal. The EPS is slower with the front \nwith strong instability and shear which could result in stronger\nthunderstorms. Trends will continue to be monitored.\n\nThe next chance of showers will be late in the week as northwest \nflow brings another front through the area.",
